{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"closures","title":"Closures","group":"Functions & Scope","order":16,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A closure is a function bundled with the variables of the scope where it was created — it keeps them alive even after that scope has returned.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Closure</strong> — the combination of a function and its <strong>lexical environment</strong>. Every JavaScript function is a closure; it becomes interesting when the function outlives the scope it was defined in.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>The basic example</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function makeCounter() {\n  let count = 0;               // private: only the returned functions can see it\n  return {\n    increment: () =&gt; ++count,\n    decrement: () =&gt; --count,\n    value: () =&gt; count,\n  };\n}\nconst a = makeCounter();\nconst b = makeCounter();       // independent environment\na.increment(); a.increment(); a.increment();\nb.decrement();\nconsole.log(a.value(), b.value(), a.count);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 -1 undefined</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tool\"></i>What closures are used for</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>Data privacy</h3><p>Variables that no outside code can touch — the <em>module pattern</em> before classes had <code>#private</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-building-factory\"></i>Function factories</h3><p><code>makeMultiplier(3)</code> returns a function that remembers <code>3</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-click\"></i>Callbacks &amp; handlers</h3><p>Event handlers and timers remember the data they were created with.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>Memoization, once, debounce</h3><p>Caches and timers kept between calls.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const makeMultiplier = (factor) =&gt; (n) =&gt; n * factor;\nconst double = makeMultiplier(2), triple = makeMultiplier(3);\nconsole.log(double(5), triple(5));\n\nfunction once(fn) {\n  let done = false, result;\n  return (...args) =&gt; {\n    if (!done) { done = true; result = fn(...args); }\n    return result;\n  };\n}\nconst init = once(() =&gt; { console.log(\"initialising…\"); return 42; });\nconsole.log(init(), init());</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 15\ninitialising…\n42 42</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-variable\"></i>Closures capture variables, not values</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>let message = \"first\";\nconst say = () =&gt; message;\nmessage = \"second\";\nconsole.log(say());          // reads the variable's CURRENT value</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>second</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bug\"></i>The loop + closure interview classic</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>for (var i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++) {\n  setTimeout(() =&gt; console.log(\"var:\", i), 0);\n}\nfor (let j = 1; j &lt;= 3; j++) {\n  setTimeout(() =&gt; console.log(\"let:\", j), 0);\n}\n// pre-ES6 fix: an IIFE creates a new scope per iteration\nfor (var k = 1; k &lt;= 3; k++) {\n  ((k) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; console.log(\"iife:\", k), 0))(k);\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>var: 4\nvar: 4\nvar: 4\nlet: 1\nlet: 2\nlet: 3\niife: 1\niife: 2\niife: 3</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-package\"></i>Module pattern with an IIFE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const bank = (function () {\n  let balance = 0;                              // private\n  function log(msg) { return `[bank] ${msg}`; } // private\n  return {\n    deposit(amount) { balance += amount; return log(`+${amount}`); },\n    get balance() { return balance; },\n  };\n})();\nconsole.log(bank.deposit(50), bank.balance, bank.log);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[bank] +50 50 undefined</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory</div><p>A closure keeps its <strong>whole</strong> environment reachable. A long-lived closure (event listener, interval, cache) that references a big object keeps that object in memory — a common leak.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is a closure? Give a practical use.</summary><div><p>A function together with references to the variables of the scope it was defined in; it can read and update them even after the outer function returned. Uses: private state (counters, module pattern), function factories, memoization caches, <code>once</code>, debounce/throttle, and preserving data in async callbacks.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does the var loop print 4, 4, 4?</summary><div><p><code>var</code> is function-scoped, so all three callbacks close over the <em>same</em> <code>i</code>. The callbacks run after the loop finished, when <code>i</code> is 4. <code>let</code> creates a new binding for each iteration, so each callback gets its own value.</p></div></details></section>"}